{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0183EPV89DMAj3KAAmN4G8pm","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: 'Original framing holds — SICA is a principled, effective method\n2\t  with formally characterizable operating regime. All 3 claims stand at full strength.\n3\t  Hero figure (C0 vs gap scatter) shows clean threshold with gradient. Paper tells\n4\t  a complete story: method + when it works + why + how to know in advance.'\n5\timagined_experiments:\n6\t- name: sica_expanded_14b\n7\t  plan_id: exp-001\n8\t  description: 'Full-scale evaluation: PW D5 600 + FOLIO 204 = 804 questions, K=12,\n9\t    T=0.7'\n10\t  expected_results:\n11\t    sica_accuracy_combined: 0.763\n12\t    sc_accuracy_combined: 0.728\n13\t    combined_gap_pp: 3.5\n14\t    folio_sica_accuracy: 0.814\n15\t    folio_sc_accuracy: 0.76\n16\t    folio_gap_pp: 5.4\n17\t    proofwriter_sica_accuracy: 0.745\n18\t    proofwriter_sc_accuracy: 0.722\n19\t    proofwriter_gap_pp: 2.3\n20\t    c0_combined: 0.43\n21\t    c0_folio: 0.48\n22\t    c0_proofwriter: 0.41\n23\t    bootstrap_p_combined: 0.018\n24\t    bootstrap_p_folio: 0.032\n25\t    bootstrap_p_proofwriter: 0.087\n26\t    total_constraints_raw: 78500\n27\t    total_constraints_unique: 22100\n28\t    maxsat_exclusion_rate: 0.031\n29\t    tiebreak_problems: 68\n30\t    tiebreak_sica_win_rate: 0.78\n31\t    wall_time_hours: 12.5\n32\t  result_rationale: D012 pilot showed +6.7pp on FOLIO (30q) and 0pp on PW (30q). At\n33\t    scale, FOLIO gain regresses toward mean but stays strong (+5.4pp) because FOLIO's\n34\t    formal logic structure aligns perfectly with FOL constraint extraction — the method's\n35\t    sweet spot. ProofWriter emerges positive (+2.3pp) because 600 questions reveal\n36\t    a long tail of genuinely ambiguous depth-5 problems where trace diversity creates\n37\t    actionable ties; the pilot's 0pp was a small-sample floor effect (30q × 67% unanimous\n38\t    = only 10 contested problems). Combined significance achieves p=0.018 because\n39\t    804 questions with 43% multi-candidate rate yields ~345 problems where SICA can\n40\t    differentiate, providing sufficient statistical power. The C0 sustains near pilot\n41\t    levels (pilot was 53%) because 14B-Instruct remains uncertain on logic reasoning\n42\t    at T=0.7 — these are genuinely difficult for a 14B instruction model.\n43\t  tables:\n44\t  - 'T1: Main Results (14B row, FOLIO + PW columns)'\n45\t  - 'T2: Tiebreak Analysis (68 tie problems, win/loss/draw)'\n46\t  figures_description:\n47\t  - 'F2 data points: (C0=0.48, gap=5.4) for FOLIO-14B, (C0=0.41, gap=2.3) for PW-14B'\n48\t  analysis: 'Core P0 result validates all three claims. FOLIO''s stronger gain than\n49\t    PW reflects domain alignment: FOLIO premises are already near-FOL, so constraint\n50\t    extraction is high-fidelity. PW depth-5 has longer chains where extraction noise\n51\t    accumulates, diluting signal. The 78 tiebreak problems with 78% SICA win rate\n52\t    directly supports Claim 1. Bootstrap p=0.018 clears significance threshold.'\n53\t- name: sica_k_ablation_14b\n54\t  plan_id: exp-002\n55\t  description: K sensitivity (K=6,8,12,16,20) on D012's 60 questions\n56\t  expected_results:\n57\t    k6_sica_accuracy: 0.717\n58\t    k6_sc_accuracy: 0.7\n59\t    k6_gap_pp: 1.7\n60\t    k6_c0: 0.38\n61\t    k6_tiebreak_freq: 0.12\n62\t    k8_sica_accuracy: 0.733\n63\t    k8_sc_accuracy: 0.7\n64\t    k8_gap_pp: 3.3\n65\t    k8_c0: 0.45\n66\t    k8_tiebreak_freq: 0.22\n67\t    k12_sica_accuracy: 0.75\n68\t    k12_sc_accuracy: 0.7\n69\t    k12_gap_pp: 5.0\n70\t    k12_c0: 0.53\n71\t    k12_tiebreak_freq: 0.3\n72\t    k16_sica_accuracy: 0.75\n73\t    k16_sc_accuracy: 0.717\n74\t    k16_gap_pp: 3.3\n75\t    k16_c0: 0.55\n76\t    k16_tiebreak_freq: 0.25\n77\t    k20_sica_accuracy: 0.75\n78\t    k20_sc_accuracy: 0.733\n79\t    k20_gap_pp: 1.7\n80\t    k20_c0: 0.57\n81\t    k20_tiebreak_freq: 0.18\n82\t    optimal_k: 12\n83\t    wall_time_hours: 4.2\n84\t  result_rationale: 'K=12 is optimal because it balances two opposing forces: (1)\n85\t    more samples increase C0 (more chances to surface minority answers), but (2) more\n86\t    samples also strengthen majority signal, reducing tie frequency. At K=6, too few\n87\t    samples → low C0 (38%) and few ties for SICA to resolve. At K=12, C0 peaks in\n88\t    utility — enough diversity to surface genuine alternatives without majority domination.\n89\t    At K=16/20, majority voting becomes so strong that ties become rare again (SC\n90\t    itself converges), and SICA''s tiebreaking advantage shrinks even though C0 is\n91\t    nominally higher. The gap curve is thus non-monotonic: rises to K=12, plateaus,\n92\t    then narrows. This makes physical sense — SICA helps most when voting is close,\n93\t    not when it''s unanimous or when majority dominates by large margin.'\n94\t  tables:\n95\t  - 'T3: K Sensitivity (5 rows × metrics)'\n96\t  figures_description:\n97\t  - 'F4: K vs accuracy curve (SICA and SC lines), with tiebreak frequency overlay'\n98\t  - 'F2 additional data points: 5 points at different K values showing C0-gap relationship\n99\t    within single model'\n100\t  analysis: 'K=12 sweet spot confirms paper''s default choice is principled, not cherry-picked.\n101\t    The non-monotonic gap pattern reveals SICA''s mechanism: it specifically resolves\n102\t    close calls. At high K, SC self-resolves most ties through volume. This insight\n103\t    strengthens the ''principled tiebreaking'' narrative — SICA is most valuable precisely\n104\t    where SC is least certain.'\n105\t- name: tiebreak_baseline_analysis\n106\t  plan_id: exp-003\n107\t  description: Compare SICA vs SC-random-tiebreak vs SC-deterministic on all available\n108\t    data\n109\t  expected_results:\n110\t    sica_tiebreak_accuracy: 0.78\n111\t    sc_random_tiebreak_accuracy: 0.5\n112\t    sc_alphabetical_accuracy: 0.55\n113\t    sc_first_seen_accuracy: 0.45\n114\t    sc_longest_chain_accuracy: 0.52\n115\t    n_tiebreak_problems: 68\n116\t    sica_vs_random_p_value: 0.003\n117\t    sica_unique_wins: 21\n118\t    sica_unique_losses: 6\n119\t    random_equivalent_problems: 41\n120\t  result_rationale: On 68 tied problems from exp-001 expanded data, SICA achieves\n121\t    78% because MaxSAT constraint weighting genuinely captures reasoning quality signal\n122\t    — traces with more satisfiable constraints tend to contain fewer logical errors.\n123\t    Random tiebreak hits 50% by definition (chance on binary/ternary outcomes). Alphabetical\n124\t    gets 55% due to mild label-answer correlation in FOLIO (True slightly more common).\n125\t    First-seen gets 45% (slight anti-correlation with generation order). The 28pp\n126\t    gap over random (78% vs 50%) on 68 problems is significant at p=0.003 (binomial\n127\t    test), providing direct evidence that SICA's tiebreaking mechanism captures real\n128\t    signal rather than getting lucky. This is the cleanest possible defense against\n129\t    the 'random baseline closes the gap' attack.\n130\t  tables:\n131\t  - 'T2: Tiebreak Comparison (5 strategies × win rate)'\n132\t  figures_description:\n133\t  - 'Bar chart: tiebreak accuracy by method, with significance brackets'\n134\t  analysis: 'The 28pp gap between SICA and random tiebreaking is the single most defensible\n135\t    number in the paper. It isolates SICA''s contribution from any sampling or dataset\n136\t    artifact. 68 tie problems give sufficient power for a binomial test. This directly\n137\t    refutes Reviewer Concern #7.'\n138\t- name: sica_7b_scaling\n139\t  plan_id: exp-004\n140\t  description: Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct on same 60 questions, testing weaker model → higher\n141\t    C0 → larger gain\n142\t  expected_results:\n143\t    sica_accuracy: 0.633\n144\t    sc_accuracy: 0.583\n145\t    gap_pp: 5.0\n146\t    c0: 0.65\n147\t    folio_sica: 0.7\n148\t    folio_sc: 0.633\n149\t    folio_gap_pp: 6.7\n150\t    proofwriter_sica: 0.6\n151\t    proofwriter_sc: 0.567\n152\t    proofwriter_gap_pp: 3.3\n153\t    bootstrap_p: 0.14\n154\t    constraints_per_problem: 72\n155\t    extraction_rate: 0.95\n156\t    exclusion_rate: 0.045\n157\t    wall_time_hours: 1.8\n158\t  result_rationale: '7B model is weaker → lower base accuracy (58% vs 70% for 14B)\n159\t    but critically higher C0 (65% vs 53%). The weaker model genuinely doesn''t know\n160\t    the answer to many problems, producing diverse candidate answers across 12 traces.\n161\t    This higher C0 gives SICA more contested problems to work with, yielding a larger\n162\t    absolute gap (+5.0pp vs +3.3pp for 14B). The mechanism is clear: 7B generates\n163\t    more traces with different answers → more ties → more opportunities for MaxSAT\n164\t    to pick the better-supported answer. Extraction rate stays high (95%) because\n165\t    Qwen-7B still follows instruction format well. Bootstrap p=0.14 on 60 questions\n166\t    isn''t significant alone, but combined with 14B data and the clear C0-gap gradient,\n167\t    it validates the predictive relationship.'\n168\t  tables:\n169\t  - 'T1: Main Results (7B row)'\n170\t  figures_description:\n171\t  - 'F2: (C0=0.65, gap=5.0) point — highest C0 in Qwen family, validates gradient'\n172\t  - 'F3: Operating Regime Heatmap (7B column shows darker ''SICA helps'' region)'\n173\t  analysis: '7B confirms the C0 gradient hypothesis within Qwen family: 7B (C0=0.65,\n174\t    gap=5.0) > 14B (C0=0.53, gap=3.3) > 72B (predicted C0~0.25, gap~1.0). This three-point\n175\t    gradient is the hero evidence for Claim 3. The lower absolute accuracy is expected\n176\t    and not a weakness — the paper''s point is that SICA helps uncertain models, not\n177\t    that it makes weak models strong.'\n178\t- name: qwq_32b_operating_regime\n179\t  plan_id: exp-005\n180\t  description: QwQ-32B negative results formatted for Operating Regime Analysis\n181\t  expected_results:\n182\t    c0_combined: 0.036\n183\t    c0_aime: 0.0\n184\t    c0_folio: 0.0\n185\t    c0_proofwriter: 0.1875\n186\t    sica_sc_gap_pp: 0.0\n187\t    n_aime: 22\n188\t    n_folio: 10\n189\t    n_proofwriter: 16\n190\t    n_combined: 48\n191\t    reasoning_model_unanimous_rate: 0.964\n192\t    conclusion: SICA≡SC when C0<5%\n193\t  result_rationale: 'QwQ-32B is a reasoning model with extended chain-of-thought that\n194\t    self-corrects during generation. Even at T=0.7, the reasoning chain converges\n195\t    to the same answer across all 12 samples because the model''s internal verification\n196\t    loop dominates stochastic sampling. C0=3.6% means only ~2 out of 48 problems ever\n197\t    produce a second candidate answer. With no ties to break, SICA degenerates to\n198\t    SC structurally — MaxSAT confirms the unanimous winner. This is the perfect negative\n199\t    control: the method correctly does nothing when there''s nothing to resolve. The\n200\t    contrast with instruction models (C0=43-65%) demonstrates that C0 reflects reasoning\n201\t    architecture, not task difficulty — same tasks, radically different C0.'\n202\t  tables:\n203\t  - 'T1: Main Results (QwQ-32B row, gap=0.0)'\n204\t  figures_description:\n205\t  - 'F2: (C0=0.036, gap=0.0) point — anchors the left side of the scatter plot'\n206\t  - 'F3: Operating Regime Heatmap (QwQ column is uniformly ''SICA=SC'' zone)'\n207\t  analysis: 'QwQ data transforms from ''failed experiment'' into strongest evidence\n208\t    for Claim 2. The C0=3.6% vs instruction model C0=43-65% gap on identical tasks\n209\t    proves that trace diversity reflects model architecture/confidence, not task properties.\n210\t    This is the key insight: ''symbolic consensus helps when there IS disagreement\n211\t    to resolve.'' Paper frames this as positive contribution, not limitation.'\n212\t- name: c0_diagnostic_validation\n213\t  plan_id: exp-006\n214\t  description: Plot C0 vs SICA-SC gap across all models and K values\n215\t  expected_results:\n216\t    n_data_points: 14\n217\t    pearson_r: 0.89\n218\t    spearman_rho: 0.91\n219\t    c0_threshold_20pct_sensitivity: 1.0\n220\t    c0_threshold_20pct_specificity: 1.0\n221\t    linear_fit_slope: 0.09\n222\t    linear_fit_intercept: -0.015\n223\t    auc_binary_classifier: 0.96\n224\t    models_included:\n225\t    - Qwen-7B\n226\t    - Qwen-14B\n227\t    - Qwen-72B\n228\t    - QwQ-32B\n229\t    - Mistral-7B\n230\t    - LLaMA-8B\n231\t    - DeepSeek-R1-8B\n232\t    k_values_included:\n233\t    - 6\n234\t    - 8\n235\t    - 12\n236\t    - 16\n237\t    - 20\n238\t  result_rationale: 'With 14 data points spanning C0 from 0.036 (QwQ) to 0.65 (Qwen-7B),\n239\t    the scatter plot shows a clean positive relationship (r=0.89) because the mechanism\n240\t    is causal: higher C0 → more ties → more SICA tiebreaking opportunities → larger\n241\t    gap. The threshold at C0=20% has perfect sensitivity/specificity because below\n242\t    20%, there simply aren''t enough multi-candidate problems for SICA to accumulate\n243\t    a measurable gap (even at 78% tiebreak accuracy, <20% ties on 60 questions = <12\n244\t    contested problems = <3 expected flips = noise). The relationship isn''t perfectly\n245\t    linear (it''s bounded above by tiebreak accuracy × tie frequency) but is monotonically\n246\t    positive across the observed range. AUC=0.96 for binary classification (''will\n247\t    SICA help?'') validates C0 as a zero-cost deployment diagnostic.'\n248\t  tables: []\n249\t  figures_description:\n250\t  - 'F2 (Hero Figure): C0 vs gap scatter, 14 points, with threshold line at C0=20%,\n251\t    linear fit, and confidence band. Points colored by model family (Qwen=blue, Mistral=orange,\n252\t    LLaMA=green, QwQ/DeepSeek=red). Clear separation between reasoning models (bottom-left\n253\t    cluster) and instruction models (upper-right spread).'\n254\t  analysis: 'This is the paper''s hero figure. The clean correlation (r=0.89) across\n255\t    3 families, 2 architectures, and 5 K values demonstrates that C0 is a universal\n256\t    predictor of SICA utility. The figure alone tells the paper''s story: check C0\n257\t    before deploying SICA. Practitioners can compute C0 from existing SC samples at\n258\t    zero additional cost.'\n259\t- name: sica_72b_awq\n260\t  plan_id: exp-007\n261\t  description: Qwen2.5-72B-Instruct AWQ 4-bit on 60 questions\n262\t  expected_results:\n263\t    sica_accuracy: 0.833\n264\t    sc_accuracy: 0.817\n265\t    gap_pp: 1.7\n266\t    c0: 0.25\n267\t    folio_sica: 0.867\n268\t    folio_sc: 0.867\n269\t    folio_gap_pp: 0.0\n270\t    proofwriter_sica: 0.833\n271\t    proofwriter_sc: 0.8\n272\t    proofwriter_gap_pp: 3.3\n273\t    bootstrap_p: 0.38\n274\t    constraints_per_problem: 95\n275\t    extraction_rate: 0.98\n276\t    wall_time_hours: 3.8\n277\t  result_rationale: '72B is a strong instruction model with high base accuracy (82%)\n278\t    but still non-reasoning, so it retains some trace diversity (C0=25%) — enough\n279\t    to stay above the 20% threshold but much lower than 14B (53%) or 7B (65%). The\n280\t    gap is small (+1.7pp) because few problems are contested, but it''s positive because\n281\t    those contested problems still benefit from MaxSAT resolution. FOLIO gap hits\n282\t    0pp because 72B gets nearly all FOLIO problems right with SC alone (87% accuracy\n283\t    → few ties). ProofWriter retains a gap because depth-5 reasoning chains remain\n284\t    challenging even for 72B. This completes the within-Qwen gradient: 7B (C0=0.65,\n285\t    +5.0pp) > 14B (C0=0.53, +3.3pp) > 72B (C0=0.25, +1.7pp), showing C0 and gap both\n286\t    decrease monotonically with scale.'\n287\t  tables:\n288\t  - 'T1: Main Results (72B row)'\n289\t  figures_description:\n290\t  - 'F2: (C0=0.25, gap=1.7) point — completes 3-point Qwen gradient'\n291\t  - 'Inset: Qwen scaling curve (3 points connected by line)'\n292\t  analysis: 72B provides the critical third point for the within-family scaling gradient.\n293\t    The monotonic C0 decrease with scale (65% → 53% → 25%) directly maps to gap decrease\n294\t    (5.0 → 3.3 → 1.7), providing causal evidence that model capability (reflected\n295\t    as lower C0) reduces SICA's opportunity. At C0=25% the model is at the boundary\n296\t    — still above threshold but barely. This supports Claim 3's 'continuous predictor'\n297\t    framing over 'binary indicator'.\n298\t- name: constraint_quality_case_studies\n299\t  plan_id: exp-008\n300\t  description: Qualitative analysis of extracted constraints on 5-10 representative\n301\t    questions\n302\t  expected_results:\n303\t    problems_analyzed: 8\n304\t    semantically_meaningful_rate: 0.85\n305\t    excluded_constraints_are_errors_rate: 0.75\n306\t    constraint_types:\n307\t    - implication\n308\t    - universal_quantification\n309\t    - negation\n310\t    - conjunction\n311\t    - disjunction\n312\t    sica_correct_sc_wrong_cases: 3\n313\t    interpretable_reason_rate: 1.0\n314\t    folio_constraint_quality: high - premises map directly to FOL\n315\t    proofwriter_constraint_quality: medium - longer chains introduce noise\n316\t  result_rationale: 'FOLIO premises are designed as near-FOL natural language, so\n317\t    constraint extraction produces semantically faithful FOL formulas 85%+ of the\n318\t    time. When MaxSAT excludes constraints, they predominantly (75%) correspond to\n319\t    traces with genuine reasoning errors — the model made a logical misstep, producing\n320\t    an inconsistent constraint set. The 3 cases where SICA corrects SC are interpretable:\n321\t    in each, the correct-answer traces produce mutually consistent constraints while\n322\t    incorrect-answer traces contain contradictions that MaxSAT identifies and downweights.\n323\t    This qualitative evidence makes the quantitative gains mechanistically interpretable\n324\t    for reviewers.'\n325\t  tables: []\n326\t  figures_description:\n327\t  - 'Case study box (Appendix): 2-3 example problems showing extracted constraints,\n328\t    MaxSAT solution, and how SICA flips SC''s wrong answer'\n329\t  analysis: Case studies provide the interpretability layer reviewers demand. They\n330\t    show SICA isn't a black box — the constraint extraction and MaxSAT resolution\n331\t    have semantically meaningful behavior. The 75% error-exclusion rate means MaxSAT\n332\t    genuinely identifies bad reasoning, not just random noise filtering.\n333\t- name: overhead_analysis\n334\t  plan_id: exp-009\n335\t  description: 'Wall-time breakdown per question: generation, constraint extraction,\n336\t    MaxSAT solving'\n337\t  expected_results:\n338\t    avg_total_time_per_problem_s: 85\n339\t    generation_time_s: 72\n340\t    generation_fraction: 0.847\n341\t    constraint_extraction_time_s: 11\n342\t    extraction_fraction: 0.129\n343\t    maxsat_solving_time_s: 2\n344\t    maxsat_fraction: 0.024\n345\t    overhead_vs_sc_pct: 15.3\n346\t    marginal_cost_per_pp_gain: 4.4s per +1pp\n347\t    gpu_cost_fraction: 0.98\n348\t    cpu_only_fraction: 0.02\n349\t  result_rationale: Generation dominates (85%) because K=12 traces with Qwen-14B at\n350\t    ~512 tokens each requires 12 forward passes through a 14B model. Constraint extraction\n351\t    adds 13% — it's an additional LLM call per trace but with a shorter prompt/response\n352\t    (extract FOL from single trace). MaxSAT solving is negligible (2%) because Z3\n353\t    solves weighted partial MaxSAT on ~20-100 constraints in milliseconds. Total overhead\n354\t    vs vanilla SC (which also needs K=12 generations) is only 15% — the extraction\n355\t    call is the only addition. This means SICA's cost is dominated by the same generation\n356\t    cost SC already pays. The 15% overhead for +3.5pp gain is a favorable trade-off\n357\t    reviewers can evaluate quantitatively.\n358\t  tables:\n359\t  - 'T4: Overhead Breakdown (generation/extraction/solving × time/fraction)'\n360\t  figures_description:\n361\t  - 'Stacked bar chart: time breakdown per component, showing generation dominates'\n362\t  analysis: The 15% overhead number is the key reviewer-facing metric. Since SC already\n363\t    requires K generations, SICA only adds constraint extraction (one additional short\n364\t    LLM call per trace). MaxSAT is free (CPU, milliseconds). This pre-empts the 'impractical\n365\t    overhead' objection — the marginal cost is one extraction call per trace, amortized\n366\t    over K=12 samples that were already being generated.\n367\t- name: sica_mistral_7b\n368\t  plan_id: exp-010\n369\t  description: Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3 on D012 60 questions, cross-family validation\n370\t  expected_results:\n371\t    sica_accuracy: 0.617\n372\t    sc_accuracy: 0.583\n373\t    gap_pp: 3.3\n374\t    c0: 0.55\n375\t    folio_sica: 0.667\n376\t    folio_sc: 0.633\n377\t    folio_gap_pp: 3.3\n378\t    proofwriter_sica: 0.6\n379\t    proofwriter_sc: 0.567\n380\t    proofwriter_gap_pp: 3.3\n381\t    bootstrap_p: 0.19\n382\t    constraints_per_problem: 58\n383\t    extraction_rate: 0.92\n384\t    exclusion_rate: 0.038\n385\t    wall_time_hours: 1.3\n386\t  result_rationale: 'Mistral-7B is a comparable-scale instruction model to Qwen-7B\n387\t    but from a completely different training pipeline (Mistral AI vs Alibaba). It\n388\t    shows C0=55% because at 7B scale, instruction models are genuinely uncertain on\n389\t    formal logic tasks regardless of training family. The gap (+3.3pp) is positive\n390\t    and within the best-tier range [1.5, 5.0] because the same mechanism applies:\n391\t    diverse traces → ties → MaxSAT resolution. Extraction rate is slightly lower (92%\n392\t    vs 95% for Qwen) because Mistral''s instruction following is marginally less consistent,\n393\t    but still high enough for the pipeline to function. Bootstrap p=0.19 on 60 questions\n394\t    isn''t significant alone, but the point is cross-family replication of the C0-gap\n395\t    pattern, not individual significance. The key finding: C0=55% (>25% threshold)\n396\t    confirms this isn''t a Qwen-specific artifact.'\n397\t  tables:\n398\t  - 'T1: Main Results (Mistral-7B row)'\n399\t  figures_description:\n400\t  - 'F2: (C0=0.55, gap=3.3) point in orange — aligns with Qwen trend line'\n401\t  - 'F3: Mistral column in heatmap'\n402\t  analysis: 'Mistral replication eliminates the single most dangerous reviewer attack:\n403\t    ''Qwen-specific artifact.'' C0=55% and positive gap demonstrate that the mechanism\n404\t    is architecture-independent — any instruction model with sufficient uncertainty\n405\t    shows the same pattern. Combined with LLaMA (exp-011), this gives 3-family coverage\n406\t    meeting EMNLP minimum bar.'\n407\t- name: sica_llama_8b\n408\t  plan_id: exp-011\n409\t  description: LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct on D012 60 questions, third family\n410\t  expected_results:\n411\t    sica_accuracy: 0.6\n412\t    sc_accuracy: 0.567\n413\t    gap_pp: 3.3\n414\t    c0: 0.52\n415\t    folio_sica: 0.633\n416\t    folio_sc: 0.6\n417\t    folio_gap_pp: 3.3\n418\t    proofwriter_sica: 0.567\n419\t    proofwriter_sc: 0.533\n420\t    proofwriter_gap_pp: 3.3\n421\t    bootstrap_p: 0.21\n422\t    constraints_per_problem: 55\n423\t    extraction_rate: 0.9\n424\t    exclusion_rate: 0.042\n425\t    wall_time_hours: 1.4\n426\t  result_rationale: LLaMA-3.1-8B is Meta's instruction-tuned model at similar scale\n427\t    to Mistral-7B. It shows C0=52% — slightly lower than Mistral (55%) because LLaMA-3.1's\n428\t    instruction tuning is somewhat more consistent, but still well above the 20% threshold.\n429\t    The +3.3pp gap confirms the pattern holds across a third completely independent\n430\t    model family. Base accuracy is slightly lower than Mistral (57% vs 58%) reflecting\n431\t    minor capability differences at this scale. Extraction rate is 90% — LLaMA's chat\n432\t    template is slightly different and occasionally produces traces that don't parse\n433\t    cleanly, but the pipeline is robust enough to handle this. The 3-family convergence\n434\t    (Qwen C0=53%, Mistral C0=55%, LLaMA C0=52% — all ~50-55% at 7-14B scale) strongly\n435\t    suggests C0 is a property of task-model capability interaction, not training data\n436\t    or architecture specifics.\n437\t  tables:\n438\t  - 'T1: Main Results (LLaMA-8B row)'\n439\t  figures_description:\n440\t  - 'F2: (C0=0.52, gap=3.3) point in green — clusters with Mistral, confirms family-independence'\n441\t  analysis: 'LLaMA provides the third family for EMNLP''s implicit ''3 model families''\n442\t    requirement. The clustering of all three 7-8B instruction models at C0≈50-55%\n443\t    with gap≈3-5pp is powerful evidence that the phenomenon is universal to instruction-tuned\n444\t    models at this capability level. Combined cross-family result: mean gap = +3.3pp,\n445\t    all C0 > 25%, all gaps positive.'\n446\t- name: gsm8k_pilot_14b\n447\t  plan_id: exp-012\n448\t  description: Qwen2.5-14B on GSM8K 30-question pilot (K=12), cross-domain test\n449\t  expected_results:\n450\t    sica_accuracy: 0.9\n451\t    sc_accuracy: 0.9\n452\t    gap_pp: 0.0\n453\t    c0: 0.1\n454\t    constraints_per_problem: 42\n455\t    extraction_rate: 0.88\n456\t    exclusion_rate: 0.02\n457\t    multi_candidate_problems: 3\n458\t    wall_time_hours: 0.8\n459\t  result_rationale: 'Qwen2.5-14B achieves ~90% on GSM8K because it''s an easy benchmark\n460\t    for this model class. With 90% accuracy and K=12, most problems have 11-12 traces\n461\t    agreeing on the correct answer → C0=10% (only 3/30 problems have any diversity).\n462\t    This low C0 is NOT because the constraint extraction fails — extraction rate is\n463\t    still 88% — but because the model simply knows the answers. SICA=SC because there\n464\t    are no ties to break. This is the ideal ''negative control for a different reason'':\n465\t    on logic tasks, 14B shows high C0 because the tasks challenge it; on arithmetic,\n466\t    the same model shows low C0 because it''s too capable. This proves C0 reflects\n467\t    task-relative difficulty, strengthening Claim 3''s ''practical diagnostic'' framing\n468\t    — if you see low C0, don''t bother with SICA regardless of domain.'\n469\t  tables:\n470\t  - 'Appendix table: GSM8K pilot results'\n471\t  figures_description:\n472\t  - 'F2: (C0=0.10, gap=0.0) point — same model as (C0=0.48, gap=5.4), different task.\n473\t    Arrow annotation: ''same model, task-dependent C0'''\n474\t  - 'F3: GSM8K column shows 14B in ''SICA=SC'' zone (contrasts with logic column)'\n475\t  analysis: 'GSM8K confirms C0 is task-relative, not model-fixed. The same 14B model\n476\t    shows C0=48% on FOLIO but C0=10% on GSM8K — proving C0 reflects model-task interaction,\n477\t    not just model weakness. This is a key insight for practitioners: compute C0 on\n478\t    YOUR task before deciding to deploy SICA. For paper, this adds the cross-domain\n479\t    dimension without requiring SICA to ''work'' on easy tasks.'\n480\t- name: constraint_ablation\n481\t  plan_id: exp-013\n482\t  description: SICA-MaxSAT vs SICA-count-only vs SICA-random-weight, CPU-only\n483\t  expected_results:\n484\t    maxsat_accuracy: 0.763\n485\t    count_only_accuracy: 0.737\n486\t    random_weight_accuracy: 0.728\n487\t    maxsat_vs_count_only_gap_pp: 2.6\n488\t    maxsat_vs_random_weight_gap_pp: 3.5\n489\t    count_only_vs_sc_gap_pp: 0.9\n490\t    maxsat_tiebreak_accuracy: 0.78\n491\t    count_only_tiebreak_accuracy: 0.62\n492\t    random_weight_tiebreak_accuracy: 0.51\n493\t    n_problems_where_methods_differ: 22\n494\t    maxsat_wins_over_count: 14\n495\t    count_wins_over_maxsat: 5\n496\t    ties_between_methods: 3\n497\t  result_rationale: 'MaxSAT beats count-only by 2.6pp because satisfiability checking\n498\t    adds genuine signal beyond constraint counting. Count-only asks ''which answer\n499\t    has more supporting constraints?'' — this captures quantity but not quality. MaxSAT\n500\t    asks ''which answer has the most internally consistent constraint set?'' — this\n501\t    captures whether the reasoning chains are logically coherent, not just numerous.\n502\t    On the 22 problems where they differ, MaxSAT wins 14 vs count-only''s 5 because\n503\t    constraint consistency correlates with reasoning correctness: a correct reasoning\n504\t    chain produces constraints that form a satisfiable set, while an incorrect chain\n505\t    often produces contradictions. Random-weight performs near SC-random (51% tiebreak\n506\t    accuracy) confirming that the weighting scheme matters. The 2.6pp gap (within\n507\t    [2.0, 5.0] best-tier range) directly refutes the ''over-engineering'' objection\n508\t    — MaxSAT''s satisfiability reasoning is genuinely load-bearing.'\n509\t  tables:\n510\t  - 'Appendix Table: Constraint Ablation (3 methods × accuracy + tiebreak performance)'\n511\t  figures_description:\n512\t  - 'Bar chart: Accuracy by method (MaxSAT > count-only > random-weight > SC-baseline)'\n513\t  analysis: This is the make-or-break experiment for Claim 1's 'principled tiebreaking\n514\t    through satisfiability' framing. The 2.6pp MaxSAT advantage over count-only proves\n515\t    that the SAT-solving machinery adds value — it's not just counting constraints,\n516\t    it's verifying their mutual consistency. If this were ≤1pp, we'd need to reframe\n517\t    as 'principled weighting.' At 2.6pp, the current framing ('satisfiability-based\n518\t    reasoning aggregation') is fully supported.\n519\t- name: sica_deepseek_r1_llama_8b\n520\t  plan_id: exp-014\n521\t  description: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-LLaMA-8B on D012 60 questions, second reasoning\n522\t    model\n523\t  expected_results:\n524\t    sica_accuracy: 0.717\n525\t    sc_accuracy: 0.717\n526\t    gap_pp: 0.0\n527\t    c0: 0.05\n528\t    folio_sica: 0.767\n529\t    folio_sc: 0.767\n530\t    folio_gap_pp: 0.0\n531\t    proofwriter_sica: 0.7\n532\t    proofwriter_sc: 0.7\n533\t    proofwriter_gap_pp: 0.0\n534\t    constraints_per_problem: 85\n535\t    extraction_rate: 0.93\n536\t    multi_candidate_problems: 3\n537\t    reasoning_trace_avg_tokens: 1800\n538\t    wall_time_hours: 1.8\n539\t  result_rationale: 'DeepSeek-R1-Distill-LLaMA-8B is a reasoning-distilled model —\n540\t    it has internalized the extended chain-of-thought pattern from R1 into an 8B LLaMA\n541\t    architecture. Despite being much smaller than QwQ-32B, it still shows C0=5% because\n542\t    the reasoning distillation teaches the model to self-verify, producing convergent\n543\t    outputs regardless of sampling temperature. Only 3/60 problems show any diversity.\n544\t    SICA=SC identically because with no ties, MaxSAT simply confirms the unanimous\n545\t    vote. This is crucial: it demonstrates that low C0 is a property of REASONING\n546\t    TRAINING (distillation/RL for chain-of-thought), not model size (QwQ is 32B, DeepSeek-R1-distill\n547\t    is 8B) or model family (QwQ is Qwen-based, this is LLaMA-based). The contrast\n548\t    with LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct (same base architecture, C0=52%) is dramatic: same\n549\t    LLaMA-8B base, but instruction-tuned vs reasoning-distilled produces C0=52% vs\n550\t    C0=5%.'\n551\t  tables:\n552\t  - 'T1: Main Results (DeepSeek-R1-8B row, gap=0.0)'\n553\t  figures_description:\n554\t  - 'F2: (C0=0.05, gap=0.0) point — clusters with QwQ at bottom-left'\n555\t  - 'Annotation arrow between LLaMA-8B-Instruct (C0=52%) and DeepSeek-R1-LLaMA-8B\n556\t    (C0=5%): ''same base, different training → different C0'''\n557\t  analysis: 'DeepSeek-R1 provides the second reasoning model negative control with\n558\t    a different base architecture. The LLaMA-8B-Instruct vs DeepSeek-R1-LLaMA-8B comparison\n559\t    is the paper''s most elegant evidence: same base model, but instruction tuning\n560\t    → C0=52% while reasoning distillation → C0=5%. This proves C0 reflects training\n561\t    regime (reasoning vs instruction), not architecture or scale. It also eliminates\n562\t    the ''QwQ is still Qwen family'' attack, since this uses LLaMA base.'\n563\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"328c8a7c-92b9-45cb-97e4-bcded209a2c9","uuid":"d0a516b7-081c-466c-bf2e-db791a28dccf","timestamp":"2026-05-16T16:25:23.628Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513_3/projects/symb_invariant_consensus/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T155754/best/review_context.yaml","content":"narrative_guidance: 'Original framing holds — SICA is a principled, effective method\n  with formally characterizable operating regime. All 3 claims stand at full strength.\n  Hero figure (C0 vs gap scatter) shows clean threshold with gradient. Paper tells\n  a complete story: method + when it works + why + how to know in advance.'\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: sica_expanded_14b\n  plan_id: exp-001\n  description: 'Full-scale evaluation: PW D5 600 + FOLIO 204 = 804 questions, K=12,\n    T=0.7'\n  expected_results:\n    sica_accuracy_combined: 0.763\n    sc_accuracy_combined: 0.728\n    combined_gap_pp: 3.5\n    folio_sica_accuracy: 0.814\n    folio_sc_accuracy: 0.76\n    folio_gap_pp: 5.4\n    proofwriter_sica_accuracy: 0.745\n    proofwriter_sc_accuracy: 0.722\n    proofwriter_gap_pp: 2.3\n    c0_combined: 0.43\n    c0_folio: 0.48\n    c0_proofwriter: 0.41\n    bootstrap_p_combined: 0.018\n    bootstrap_p_folio: 0.032\n    bootstrap_p_proofwriter: 0.087\n    total_constraints_raw: 78500\n    total_constraints_unique: 22100\n    maxsat_exclusion_rate: 0.031\n    tiebreak_problems: 68\n    tiebreak_sica_win_rate: 0.78\n    wall_time_hours: 12.5\n  result_rationale: D012 pilot showed +6.7pp on FOLIO (30q) and 0pp on PW (30q). At\n    scale, FOLIO gain regresses toward mean but stays strong (+5.4pp) because FOLIO's\n    formal logic structure aligns perfectly with FOL constraint extraction — the method's\n    sweet spot. ProofWriter emerges positive (+2.3pp) because 600 questions reveal\n    a long tail of genuinely ambiguous depth-5 problems where trace diversity creates\n    actionable ties; the pilot's 0pp was a small-sample floor effect (30q × 67% unanimous\n    = only 10 contested problems). Combined significance achieves p=0.018 because\n    804 questions with 43% multi-candidate rate yields ~345 problems where SICA can\n    differentiate, providing sufficient statistical power. The C0 sustains near pilot\n    levels (pilot was 53%) because 14B-Instruct remains uncertain on logic reasoning\n    at T=0.7 — these are genuinely difficult for a 14B instruction model.\n  tables:\n  - 'T1: Main Results (14B row, FOLIO + PW columns)'\n  - 'T2: Tiebreak Analysis (68 tie problems, win/loss/draw)'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'F2 data points: (C0=0.48, gap=5.4) for FOLIO-14B, (C0=0.41, gap=2.3) for PW-14B'\n  analysis: 'Core P0 result validates all three claims. FOLIO''s stronger gain than\n    PW reflects domain alignment: FOLIO premises are already near-FOL, so constraint\n    extraction is high-fidelity. PW depth-5 has longer chains where extraction noise\n    accumulates, diluting signal. The 78 tiebreak problems with 78% SICA win rate\n    directly supports Claim 1. Bootstrap p=0.018 clears significance threshold.'\n- name: sica_k_ablation_14b\n  plan_id: exp-002\n  description: K sensitivity (K=6,8,12,16,20) on D012's 60 questions\n  expected_results:\n    k6_sica_accuracy: 0.717\n    k6_sc_accuracy: 0.7\n    k6_gap_pp: 1.7\n    k6_c0: 0.38\n    k6_tiebreak_freq: 0.12\n    k8_sica_accuracy: 0.733\n    k8_sc_accuracy: 0.7\n    k8_gap_pp: 3.3\n    k8_c0: 0.45\n    k8_tiebreak_freq: 0.22\n    k12_sica_accuracy: 0.75\n    k12_sc_accuracy: 0.7\n    k12_gap_pp: 5.0\n    k12_c0: 0.53\n    k12_tiebreak_freq: 0.3\n    k16_sica_accuracy: 0.75\n    k16_sc_accuracy: 0.717\n    k16_gap_pp: 3.3\n    k16_c0: 0.55\n    k16_tiebreak_freq: 0.25\n    k20_sica_accuracy: 0.75\n    k20_sc_accuracy: 0.733\n    k20_gap_pp: 1.7\n    k20_c0: 0.57\n    k20_tiebreak_freq: 0.18\n    optimal_k: 12\n    wall_time_hours: 4.2\n  result_rationale: 'K=12 is optimal because it balances two opposing forces: (1)\n    more samples increase C0 (more chances to surface minority answers), but (2) more\n    samples also strengthen majority signal, reducing tie frequency. At K=6, too few\n    samples → low C0 (38%) and few ties for SICA to resolve. At K=12, C0 peaks in\n    utility — enough diversity to surface genuine alternatives without majority domination.\n    At K=16/20, majority voting becomes so strong that ties become rare again (SC\n    itself converges), and SICA''s tiebreaking advantage shrinks even though C0 is\n    nominally higher. The gap curve is thus non-monotonic: rises to K=12, plateaus,\n    then narrows. This makes physical sense — SICA helps most when voting is close,\n    not when it''s unanimous or when majority dominates by large margin.'\n  tables:\n  - 'T3: K Sensitivity (5 rows × metrics)'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'F4: K vs accuracy curve (SICA and SC lines), with tiebreak frequency overlay'\n  - 'F2 additional data points: 5 points at different K values showing C0-gap relationship\n    within single model'\n  analysis: 'K=12 sweet spot confirms paper''s default choice is principled, not cherry-picked.\n    The non-monotonic gap pattern reveals SICA''s mechanism: it specifically resolves\n    close calls. At high K, SC self-resolves most ties through volume. This insight\n    strengthens the ''principled tiebreaking'' narrative — SICA is most valuable precisely\n    where SC is least certain.'\n- name: tiebreak_baseline_analysis\n  plan_id: exp-003\n  description: Compare SICA vs SC-random-tiebreak vs SC-deterministic on all available\n    data\n  expected_results:\n    sica_tiebreak_accuracy: 0.78\n    sc_random_tiebreak_accuracy: 0.5\n    sc_alphabetical_accuracy: 0.55\n    sc_first_seen_accuracy: 0.45\n    sc_longest_chain_accuracy: 0.52\n    n_tiebreak_problems: 68\n    sica_vs_random_p_value: 0.003\n    sica_unique_wins: 21\n    sica_unique_losses: 6\n    random_equivalent_problems: 41\n  result_rationale: On 68 tied problems from exp-001 expanded data, SICA achieves\n    78% because MaxSAT constraint weighting genuinely captures reasoning quality signal\n    — traces with more satisfiable constraints tend to contain fewer logical errors.\n    Random tiebreak hits 50% by definition (chance on binary/ternary outcomes). Alphabetical\n    gets 55% due to mild label-answer correlation in FOLIO (True slightly more common).\n    First-seen gets 45% (slight anti-correlation with generation order). The 28pp\n    gap over random (78% vs 50%) on 68 problems is significant at p=0.003 (binomial\n    test), providing direct evidence that SICA's tiebreaking mechanism captures real\n    signal rather than getting lucky. This is the cleanest possible defense against\n    the 'random baseline closes the gap' attack.\n  tables:\n  - 'T2: Tiebreak Comparison (5 strategies × win rate)'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Bar chart: tiebreak accuracy by method, with significance brackets'\n  analysis: 'The 28pp gap between SICA and random tiebreaking is the single most defensible\n    number in the paper. It isolates SICA''s contribution from any sampling or dataset\n    artifact. 68 tie problems give sufficient power for a binomial test. This directly\n    refutes Reviewer Concern #7.'\n- name: sica_7b_scaling\n  plan_id: exp-004\n  description: Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct on same 60 questions, testing weaker model → higher\n    C0 → larger gain\n  expected_results:\n    sica_accuracy: 0.633\n    sc_accuracy: 0.583\n    gap_pp: 5.0\n    c0: 0.65\n    folio_sica: 0.7\n    folio_sc: 0.633\n    folio_gap_pp: 6.7\n    proofwriter_sica: 0.6\n    proofwriter_sc: 0.567\n    proofwriter_gap_pp: 3.3\n    bootstrap_p: 0.14\n    constraints_per_problem: 72\n    extraction_rate: 0.95\n    exclusion_rate: 0.045\n    wall_time_hours: 1.8\n  result_rationale: '7B model is weaker → lower base accuracy (58% vs 70% for 14B)\n    but critically higher C0 (65% vs 53%). The weaker model genuinely doesn''t know\n    the answer to many problems, producing diverse candidate answers across 12 traces.\n    This higher C0 gives SICA more contested problems to work with, yielding a larger\n    absolute gap (+5.0pp vs +3.3pp for 14B). The mechanism is clear: 7B generates\n    more traces with different answers → more ties → more opportunities for MaxSAT\n    to pick the better-supported answer. Extraction rate stays high (95%) because\n    Qwen-7B still follows instruction format well. Bootstrap p=0.14 on 60 questions\n    isn''t significant alone, but combined with 14B data and the clear C0-gap gradient,\n    it validates the predictive relationship.'\n  tables:\n  - 'T1: Main Results (7B row)'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'F2: (C0=0.65, gap=5.0) point — highest C0 in Qwen family, validates gradient'\n  - 'F3: Operating Regime Heatmap (7B column shows darker ''SICA helps'' region)'\n  analysis: '7B confirms the C0 gradient hypothesis within Qwen family: 7B (C0=0.65,\n    gap=5.0) > 14B (C0=0.53, gap=3.3) > 72B (predicted C0~0.25, gap~1.0). This three-point\n    gradient is the hero evidence for Claim 3. The lower absolute accuracy is expected\n    and not a weakness — the paper''s point is that SICA helps uncertain models, not\n    that it makes weak models strong.'\n- name: qwq_32b_operating_regime\n  plan_id: exp-005\n  description: QwQ-32B negative results formatted for Operating Regime Analysis\n  expected_results:\n    c0_combined: 0.036\n    c0_aime: 0.0\n    c0_folio: 0.0\n    c0_proofwriter: 0.1875\n    sica_sc_gap_pp: 0.0\n    n_aime: 22\n    n_folio: 10\n    n_proofwriter: 16\n    n_combined: 48\n    reasoning_model_unanimous_rate: 0.964\n    conclusion: SICA≡SC when C0<5%\n  result_rationale: 'QwQ-32B is a reasoning model with extended chain-of-thought that\n    self-corrects during generation. Even at T=0.7, the reasoning chain converges\n    to the same answer across all 12 samples because the model''s internal verification\n    loop dominates stochastic sampling. C0=3.6% means only ~2 out of 48 problems ever\n    produce a second candidate answer. With no ties to break, SICA degenerates to\n    SC structurally — MaxSAT confirms the unanimous winner. This is the perfect negative\n    control: the method correctly does nothing when there''s nothing to resolve. The\n    contrast with instruction models (C0=43-65%) demonstrates that C0 reflects reasoning\n    architecture, not task difficulty — same tasks, radically different C0.'\n  tables:\n  - 'T1: Main Results (QwQ-32B row, gap=0.0)'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'F2: (C0=0.036, gap=0.0) point — anchors the left side of the scatter plot'\n  - 'F3: Operating Regime Heatmap (QwQ column is uniformly ''SICA=SC'' zone)'\n  analysis: 'QwQ data transforms from ''failed experiment'' into strongest evidence\n    for Claim 2. The C0=3.6% vs instruction model C0=43-65% gap on identical tasks\n    proves that trace diversity reflects model architecture/confidence, not task properties.\n    This is the key insight: ''symbolic consensus helps when there IS disagreement\n    to resolve.'' Paper frames this as positive contribution, not limitation.'\n- name: c0_diagnostic_validation\n  plan_id: exp-006\n  description: Plot C0 vs SICA-SC gap across all models and K values\n  expected_results:\n    n_data_points: 14\n    pearson_r: 0.89\n    spearman_rho: 0.91\n    c0_threshold_20pct_sensitivity: 1.0\n    c0_threshold_20pct_specificity: 1.0\n    linear_fit_slope: 0.09\n    linear_fit_intercept: -0.015\n    auc_binary_classifier: 0.96\n    models_included:\n    - Qwen-7B\n    - Qwen-14B\n    - Qwen-72B\n    - QwQ-32B\n    - Mistral-7B\n    - LLaMA-8B\n    - DeepSeek-R1-8B\n    k_values_included:\n    - 6\n    - 8\n    - 12\n    - 16\n    - 20\n  result_rationale: 'With 14 data points spanning C0 from 0.036 (QwQ) to 0.65 (Qwen-7B),\n    the scatter plot shows a clean positive relationship (r=0.89) because the mechanism\n    is causal: higher C0 → more ties → more SICA tiebreaking opportunities → larger\n    gap. The threshold at C0=20% has perfect sensitivity/specificity because below\n    20%, there simply aren''t enough multi-candidate problems for SICA to accumulate\n    a measurable gap (even at 78% tiebreak accuracy, <20% ties on 60 questions = <12\n    contested problems = <3 expected flips = noise). The relationship isn''t perfectly\n    linear (it''s bounded above by tiebreak accuracy × tie frequency) but is monotonically\n    positive across the observed range. AUC=0.96 for binary classification (''will\n    SICA help?'') validates C0 as a zero-cost deployment diagnostic.'\n  tables: []\n  figures_description:\n  - 'F2 (Hero Figure): C0 vs gap scatter, 14 points, with threshold line at C0=20%,\n    linear fit, and confidence band. Points colored by model family (Qwen=blue, Mistral=orange,\n    LLaMA=green, QwQ/DeepSeek=red). Clear separation between reasoning models (bottom-left\n    cluster) and instruction models (upper-right spread).'\n  analysis: 'This is the paper''s hero figure. The clean correlation (r=0.89) across\n    3 families, 2 architectures, and 5 K values demonstrates that C0 is a universal\n    predictor of SICA utility. The figure alone tells the paper''s story: check C0\n    before deploying SICA. Practitioners can compute C0 from existing SC samples at\n    zero additional cost.'\n- name: sica_72b_awq\n  plan_id: exp-007\n  description: Qwen2.5-72B-Instruct AWQ 4-bit on 60 questions\n  expected_results:\n    sica_accuracy: 0.833\n    sc_accuracy: 0.817\n    gap_pp: 1.7\n    c0: 0.25\n    folio_sica: 0.867\n    folio_sc: 0.867\n    folio_gap_pp: 0.0\n    proofwriter_sica: 0.833\n    proofwriter_sc: 0.8\n    proofwriter_gap_pp: 3.3\n    bootstrap_p: 0.38\n    constraints_per_problem: 95\n    extraction_rate: 0.98\n    wall_time_hours: 3.8\n  result_rationale: '72B is a strong instruction model with high base accuracy (82%)\n    but still non-reasoning, so it retains some trace diversity (C0=25%) — enough\n    to stay above the 20% threshold but much lower than 14B (53%) or 7B (65%). The\n    gap is small (+1.7pp) because few problems are contested, but it''s positive because\n    those contested problems still benefit from MaxSAT resolution. FOLIO gap hits\n    0pp because 72B gets nearly all FOLIO problems right with SC alone (87% accuracy\n    → few ties). ProofWriter retains a gap because depth-5 reasoning chains remain\n    challenging even for 72B. This completes the within-Qwen gradient: 7B (C0=0.65,\n    +5.0pp) > 14B (C0=0.53, +3.3pp) > 72B (C0=0.25, +1.7pp), showing C0 and gap both\n    decrease monotonically with scale.'\n  tables:\n  - 'T1: Main Results (72B row)'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'F2: (C0=0.25, gap=1.7) point — completes 3-point Qwen gradient'\n  - 'Inset: Qwen scaling curve (3 points connected by line)'\n  analysis: 72B provides the critical third point for the within-family scaling gradient.\n    The monotonic C0 decrease with scale (65% → 53% → 25%) directly maps to gap decrease\n    (5.0 → 3.3 → 1.7), providing causal evidence that model capability (reflected\n    as lower C0) reduces SICA's opportunity. At C0=25% the model is at the boundary\n    — still above threshold but barely. This supports Claim 3's 'continuous predictor'\n    framing over 'binary indicator'.\n- name: constraint_quality_case_studies\n  plan_id: exp-008\n  description: Qualitative analysis of extracted constraints on 5-10 representative\n    questions\n  expected_results:\n    problems_analyzed: 8\n    semantically_meaningful_rate: 0.85\n    excluded_constraints_are_errors_rate: 0.75\n    constraint_types:\n    - implication\n    - universal_quantification\n    - negation\n    - conjunction\n    - disjunction\n    sica_correct_sc_wrong_cases: 3\n    interpretable_reason_rate: 1.0\n    folio_constraint_quality: high - premises map directly to FOL\n    proofwriter_constraint_quality: medium - longer chains introduce noise\n  result_rationale: 'FOLIO premises are designed as near-FOL natural language, so\n    constraint extraction produces semantically faithful FOL formulas 85%+ of the\n    time. When MaxSAT excludes constraints, they predominantly (75%) correspond to\n    traces with genuine reasoning errors — the model made a logical misstep, producing\n    an inconsistent constraint set. The 3 cases where SICA corrects SC are interpretable:\n    in each, the correct-answer traces produce mutually consistent constraints while\n    incorrect-answer traces contain contradictions that MaxSAT identifies and downweights.\n    This qualitative evidence makes the quantitative gains mechanistically interpretable\n    for reviewers.'\n  tables: []\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Case study box (Appendix): 2-3 example problems showing extracted constraints,\n    MaxSAT solution, and how SICA flips SC''s wrong answer'\n  analysis: Case studies provide the interpretability layer reviewers demand. They\n    show SICA isn't a black box — the constraint extraction and MaxSAT resolution\n    have semantically meaningful behavior. The 75% error-exclusion rate means MaxSAT\n    genuinely identifies bad reasoning, not just random noise filtering.\n- name: overhead_analysis\n  plan_id: exp-009\n  description: 'Wall-time breakdown per question: generation, constraint extraction,\n    MaxSAT solving'\n  expected_results:\n    avg_total_time_per_problem_s: 85\n    generation_time_s: 72\n    generation_fraction: 0.847\n    constraint_extraction_time_s: 11\n    extraction_fraction: 0.129\n    maxsat_solving_time_s: 2\n    maxsat_fraction: 0.024\n    overhead_vs_sc_pct: 15.3\n    marginal_cost_per_pp_gain: 4.4s per +1pp\n    gpu_cost_fraction: 0.98\n    cpu_only_fraction: 0.02\n  result_rationale: Generation dominates (85%) because K=12 traces with Qwen-14B at\n    ~512 tokens each requires 12 forward passes through a 14B model. Constraint extraction\n    adds 13% — it's an additional LLM call per trace but with a shorter prompt/response\n    (extract FOL from single trace). MaxSAT solving is negligible (2%) because Z3\n    solves weighted partial MaxSAT on ~20-100 constraints in milliseconds. Total overhead\n    vs vanilla SC (which also needs K=12 generations) is only 15% — the extraction\n    call is the only addition. This means SICA's cost is dominated by the same generation\n    cost SC already pays. The 15% overhead for +3.5pp gain is a favorable trade-off\n    reviewers can evaluate quantitatively.\n  tables:\n  - 'T4: Overhead Breakdown (generation/extraction/solving × time/fraction)'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Stacked bar chart: time breakdown per component, showing generation dominates'\n  analysis: The 15% overhead number is the key reviewer-facing metric. Since SC already\n    requires K generations, SICA only adds constraint extraction (one additional short\n    LLM call per trace). MaxSAT is free (CPU, milliseconds). This pre-empts the 'impractical\n    overhead' objection — the marginal cost is one extraction call per trace, amortized\n    over K=12 samples that were already being generated.\n- name: sica_mistral_7b\n  plan_id: exp-010\n  description: Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3 on D012 60 questions, cross-family validation\n  expected_results:\n    sica_accuracy: 0.617\n    sc_accuracy: 0.583\n    gap_pp: 3.3\n    c0: 0.55\n    folio_sica: 0.667\n    folio_sc: 0.633\n    folio_gap_pp: 3.3\n    proofwriter_sica: 0.6\n    proofwriter_sc: 0.567\n    proofwriter_gap_pp: 3.3\n    bootstrap_p: 0.19\n    constraints_per_problem: 58\n    extraction_rate: 0.92\n    exclusion_rate: 0.038\n    wall_time_hours: 1.3\n  result_rationale: 'Mistral-7B is a comparable-scale instruction model to Qwen-7B\n    but from a completely different training pipeline (Mistral AI vs Alibaba). It\n    shows C0=55% because at 7B scale, instruction models are genuinely uncertain on\n    formal logic tasks regardless of training family. The gap (+3.3pp) is positive\n    and within the best-tier range [1.5, 5.0] because the same mechanism applies:\n    diverse traces → ties → MaxSAT resolution. Extraction rate is slightly lower (92%\n    vs 95% for Qwen) because Mistral''s instruction following is marginally less consistent,\n    but still high enough for the pipeline to function. Bootstrap p=0.19 on 60 questions\n    isn''t significant alone, but the point is cross-family replication of the C0-gap\n    pattern, not individual significance. The key finding: C0=55% (>25% threshold)\n    confirms this isn''t a Qwen-specific artifact.'\n  tables:\n  - 'T1: Main Results (Mistral-7B row)'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'F2: (C0=0.55, gap=3.3) point in orange — aligns with Qwen trend line'\n  - 'F3: Mistral column in heatmap'\n  analysis: 'Mistral replication eliminates the single most dangerous reviewer attack:\n    ''Qwen-specific artifact.'' C0=55% and positive gap demonstrate that the mechanism\n    is architecture-independent — any instruction model with sufficient uncertainty\n    shows the same pattern. Combined with LLaMA (exp-011), this gives 3-family coverage\n    meeting EMNLP minimum bar.'\n- name: sica_llama_8b\n  plan_id: exp-011\n  description: LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct on D012 60 questions, third family\n  expected_results:\n    sica_accuracy: 0.6\n    sc_accuracy: 0.567\n    gap_pp: 3.3\n    c0: 0.52\n    folio_sica: 0.633\n    folio_sc: 0.6\n    folio_gap_pp: 3.3\n    proofwriter_sica: 0.567\n    proofwriter_sc: 0.533\n    proofwriter_gap_pp: 3.3\n    bootstrap_p: 0.21\n    constraints_per_problem: 55\n    extraction_rate: 0.9\n    exclusion_rate: 0.042\n    wall_time_hours: 1.4\n  result_rationale: LLaMA-3.1-8B is Meta's instruction-tuned model at similar scale\n    to Mistral-7B. It shows C0=52% — slightly lower than Mistral (55%) because LLaMA-3.1's\n    instruction tuning is somewhat more consistent, but still well above the 20% threshold.\n    The +3.3pp gap confirms the pattern holds across a third completely independent\n    model family. Base accuracy is slightly lower than Mistral (57% vs 58%) reflecting\n    minor capability differences at this scale. Extraction rate is 90% — LLaMA's chat\n    template is slightly different and occasionally produces traces that don't parse\n    cleanly, but the pipeline is robust enough to handle this. The 3-family convergence\n    (Qwen C0=53%, Mistral C0=55%, LLaMA C0=52% — all ~50-55% at 7-14B scale) strongly\n    suggests C0 is a property of task-model capability interaction, not training data\n    or architecture specifics.\n  tables:\n  - 'T1: Main Results (LLaMA-8B row)'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'F2: (C0=0.52, gap=3.3) point in green — clusters with Mistral, confirms family-independence'\n  analysis: 'LLaMA provides the third family for EMNLP''s implicit ''3 model families''\n    requirement. The clustering of all three 7-8B instruction models at C0≈50-55%\n    with gap≈3-5pp is powerful evidence that the phenomenon is universal to instruction-tuned\n    models at this capability level. Combined cross-family result: mean gap = +3.3pp,\n    all C0 > 25%, all gaps positive.'\n- name: gsm8k_pilot_14b\n  plan_id: exp-012\n  description: Qwen2.5-14B on GSM8K 30-question pilot (K=12), cross-domain test\n  expected_results:\n    sica_accuracy: 0.9\n    sc_accuracy: 0.9\n    gap_pp: 0.0\n    c0: 0.1\n    constraints_per_problem: 42\n    extraction_rate: 0.88\n    exclusion_rate: 0.02\n    multi_candidate_problems: 3\n    wall_time_hours: 0.8\n  result_rationale: 'Qwen2.5-14B achieves ~90% on GSM8K because it''s an easy benchmark\n    for this model class. With 90% accuracy and K=12, most problems have 11-12 traces\n    agreeing on the correct answer → C0=10% (only 3/30 problems have any diversity).\n    This low C0 is NOT because the constraint extraction fails — extraction rate is\n    still 88% — but because the model simply knows the answers. SICA=SC because there\n    are no ties to break. This is the ideal ''negative control for a different reason'':\n    on logic tasks, 14B shows high C0 because the tasks challenge it; on arithmetic,\n    the same model shows low C0 because it''s too capable. This proves C0 reflects\n    task-relative difficulty, strengthening Claim 3''s ''practical diagnostic'' framing\n    — if you see low C0, don''t bother with SICA regardless of domain.'\n  tables:\n  - 'Appendix table: GSM8K pilot results'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'F2: (C0=0.10, gap=0.0) point — same model as (C0=0.48, gap=5.4), different task.\n    Arrow annotation: ''same model, task-dependent C0'''\n  - 'F3: GSM8K column shows 14B in ''SICA=SC'' zone (contrasts with logic column)'\n  analysis: 'GSM8K confirms C0 is task-relative, not model-fixed. The same 14B model\n    shows C0=48% on FOLIO but C0=10% on GSM8K — proving C0 reflects model-task interaction,\n    not just model weakness. This is a key insight for practitioners: compute C0 on\n    YOUR task before deciding to deploy SICA. For paper, this adds the cross-domain\n    dimension without requiring SICA to ''work'' on easy tasks.'\n- name: constraint_ablation\n  plan_id: exp-013\n  description: SICA-MaxSAT vs SICA-count-only vs SICA-random-weight, CPU-only\n  expected_results:\n    maxsat_accuracy: 0.763\n    count_only_accuracy: 0.737\n    random_weight_accuracy: 0.728\n    maxsat_vs_count_only_gap_pp: 2.6\n    maxsat_vs_random_weight_gap_pp: 3.5\n    count_only_vs_sc_gap_pp: 0.9\n    maxsat_tiebreak_accuracy: 0.78\n    count_only_tiebreak_accuracy: 0.62\n    random_weight_tiebreak_accuracy: 0.51\n    n_problems_where_methods_differ: 22\n    maxsat_wins_over_count: 14\n    count_wins_over_maxsat: 5\n    ties_between_methods: 3\n  result_rationale: 'MaxSAT beats count-only by 2.6pp because satisfiability checking\n    adds genuine signal beyond constraint counting. Count-only asks ''which answer\n    has more supporting constraints?'' — this captures quantity but not quality. MaxSAT\n    asks ''which answer has the most internally consistent constraint set?'' — this\n    captures whether the reasoning chains are logically coherent, not just numerous.\n    On the 22 problems where they differ, MaxSAT wins 14 vs count-only''s 5 because\n    constraint consistency correlates with reasoning correctness: a correct reasoning\n    chain produces constraints that form a satisfiable set, while an incorrect chain\n    often produces contradictions. Random-weight performs near SC-random (51% tiebreak\n    accuracy) confirming that the weighting scheme matters. The 2.6pp gap (within\n    [2.0, 5.0] best-tier range) directly refutes the ''over-engineering'' objection\n    — MaxSAT''s satisfiability reasoning is genuinely load-bearing.'\n  tables:\n  - 'Appendix Table: Constraint Ablation (3 methods × accuracy + tiebreak performance)'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Bar chart: Accuracy by method (MaxSAT > count-only > random-weight > SC-baseline)'\n  analysis: This is the make-or-break experiment for Claim 1's 'principled tiebreaking\n    through satisfiability' framing. The 2.6pp MaxSAT advantage over count-only proves\n    that the SAT-solving machinery adds value — it's not just counting constraints,\n    it's verifying their mutual consistency. If this were ≤1pp, we'd need to reframe\n    as 'principled weighting.' At 2.6pp, the current framing ('satisfiability-based\n    reasoning aggregation') is fully supported.\n- name: sica_deepseek_r1_llama_8b\n  plan_id: exp-014\n  description: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-LLaMA-8B on D012 60 questions, second reasoning\n    model\n  expected_results:\n    sica_accuracy: 0.717\n    sc_accuracy: 0.717\n    gap_pp: 0.0\n    c0: 0.05\n    folio_sica: 0.767\n    folio_sc: 0.767\n    folio_gap_pp: 0.0\n    proofwriter_sica: 0.7\n    proofwriter_sc: 0.7\n    proofwriter_gap_pp: 0.0\n    constraints_per_problem: 85\n    extraction_rate: 0.93\n    multi_candidate_problems: 3\n    reasoning_trace_avg_tokens: 1800\n    wall_time_hours: 1.8\n  result_rationale: 'DeepSeek-R1-Distill-LLaMA-8B is a reasoning-distilled model —\n    it has internalized the extended chain-of-thought pattern from R1 into an 8B LLaMA\n    architecture. Despite being much smaller than QwQ-32B, it still shows C0=5% because\n    the reasoning distillation teaches the model to self-verify, producing convergent\n    outputs regardless of sampling temperature. Only 3/60 problems show any diversity.\n    SICA=SC identically because with no ties, MaxSAT simply confirms the unanimous\n    vote. This is crucial: it demonstrates that low C0 is a property of REASONING\n    TRAINING (distillation/RL for chain-of-thought), not model size (QwQ is 32B, DeepSeek-R1-distill\n    is 8B) or model family (QwQ is Qwen-based, this is LLaMA-based). The contrast\n    with LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct (same base architecture, C0=52%) is dramatic: same\n    LLaMA-8B base, but instruction-tuned vs reasoning-distilled produces C0=52% vs\n    C0=5%.'\n  tables:\n  - 'T1: Main Results (DeepSeek-R1-8B row, gap=0.0)'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'F2: (C0=0.05, gap=0.0) point — clusters with QwQ at bottom-left'\n  - 'Annotation arrow between LLaMA-8B-Instruct (C0=52%) and DeepSeek-R1-LLaMA-8B\n    (C0=5%): ''same base, different training → different C0'''\n  analysis: 'DeepSeek-R1 provides the second reasoning model negative control with\n    a different base architecture. The LLaMA-8B-Instruct vs DeepSeek-R1-LLaMA-8B comparison\n    is the paper''s most elegant evidence: same base model, but instruction tuning\n    → C0=52% while reasoning distillation → C0=5%. This proves C0 reflects training\n    regime (reasoning vs instruction), not architecture or scale. It also eliminates\n    the ''QwQ is still Qwen family'' attack, since this uses LLaMA base.'\n","numLines":563,"startLine":1,"totalLines":563}}}
